examples.question_text:
The graph shows a cubic $y=f(x)$. Its stationary points occur at $x=-2$ and $x=2$, and its only point of inflection occurs at $x=0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$f(x)$},xmin=-4.3,xmax=4.3,ymin=-22,ymax=22,xtick={-2,2},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=120]
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-4:4] {x*x*x-12*x};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:-2,0)--(axis cs:-2,16);
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:2,0)--(axis cs:2,-16);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

a. State where $f$ is increasing and decreasing.
b. State where $f$ is concave up and concave down.

examples.solution_text:
The domain is $\mathbb R$. The intercepts are $(0,0)$ and $(6,0)$. The double root at $x=6$ means the curve touches the axis there.

$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=(x-6)^2+2x(x-6)\\&=(x-6)(3x-6)\\&=3(x-2)(x-6)\end{aligned}$

For stationary points:

$\begin{aligned}3(x-2)(x-6)&=0\\x&=2,\ 6\end{aligned}$

| $x$ | $x<2$ | $2$ | $2<x<6$ | $6$ | $x>6$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $f'(x)$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |
| Behaviour | increasing | maximum | decreasing | minimum | increasing |

$\begin{aligned}f(2)&=2(2-6)^2=32\\f(6)&=0\\f''(x)&=6x-24\end{aligned}$

The maximum is $(2,32)$ and the minimum is $(6,0)$.

$\begin{aligned}6x-24&=0\\x&=4\\f(4)&=4(4-6)^2=16\end{aligned}$

Since $f''$ changes from negative to positive at $x=4$, $(4,16)$ is a point of inflection. It is non-horizontal because $f'(4)=-12$.

The leading term is $x^3$, so $f(x)\to-\infty$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $f(x)\to\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-1.1,xmax=8.1,ymin=-18,ymax=43,xtick={2,4,6},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=140]
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-0.4:7.8] {x*(x-6)*(x-6)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above] at (axis cs:2,32) {$(2,32)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above right] at (axis cs:4,16) {$(4,16)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below right] at (axis cs:6,0) {$(6,0)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below right] at (axis cs:0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,0) (2,32) (4,16) (6,0)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The domain is $\mathbb R$. Since $f(x)=x^3(x-8)$, the intercepts are $(0,0)$ and $(8,0)$. Both ends rise because the leading term is $x^4$.

$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=4x^3-24x^2\\&=4x^2(x-6)\\f''(x)&=12x^2-48x\\&=12x(x-4)\end{aligned}$

Stationary points occur at $x=0$ and $x=6$.

$\begin{aligned}f(0)&=0\\f(6)&=6^4-8(6^3)=-432\\f''(0)&=0\\f''(6)&=144>0\end{aligned}$

Thus $(6,-432)$ is a minimum. The second derivative test is inconclusive at $x=0$.

| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| $f'(x)$ | $-28$ | $0$ | $-20$ |

The curve is decreasing on both sides of $x=0$, so this stationary point is not a turning point.

For possible inflections, $12x(x-4)=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=4$.

| $x$ | $x<0$ | $0$ | $0<x<4$ | $4$ | $x>4$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $f''(x)$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |

Concavity changes at both values. The points of inflection are $(0,0)$ and $(4,-256)$. The first is horizontal; the second is non-horizontal since $f'(4)=-128$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-3,xmax=9,ymin=-530,ymax=180,xtick={4,6,8},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=160]
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.3:8.3] {x*x*x*x-8*x*x*x};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,0) (4,-256) (6,-432) (8,0)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above right] at (axis cs:0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below left] at (axis cs:4,-256) {$(4,-256)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below] at (axis cs:6,-432) {$(6,-432)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above left] at (axis cs:8,0) {$(8,0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The domain is $\mathbb R\setminus\{-3,3\}$. Since $f(-x)=f(x)$, the graph is symmetric in the $y$-axis. There are no $x$-intercepts, and the $y$-intercept is $\left(0,-\dfrac19\right)$.

$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=-\frac{2x}{(x^2-9)^2}\\f''(x)&=\frac{6(x^2+3)}{(x^2-9)^3}\end{aligned}$

The only stationary point occurs at $x=0$. The sign of $f'$ changes from positive to negative there, so $\left(0,-\dfrac19\right)$ is a local maximum.

The curve is concave up for $x<-3$ or $x>3$, and concave down for $-3<x<3$. There are no points of inflection: the numerator of $f''$ never vanishes, and $x=\pm3$ are outside the domain.

The vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=3$.

| Approach | $x\to-3^-$ | $x\to-3^+$ | $x\to3^-$ | $x\to3^+$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| $f(x)$ | $\infty$ | $-\infty$ | $-\infty$ | $\infty$ |

As $x\to\pm\infty$, $f(x)\to0^+$, so the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-7,xmax=7,ymin=-0.8,ymax=0.8,xtick={-3,3},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=150]
\addplot[black,dashed] coordinates {(-3,-0.8) (-3,0.8)};
\addplot[black,dashed] coordinates {(3,-0.8) (3,0.8)};
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-6.7:-3.22] {1/(x*x-9)};
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-2.77:2.77] {1/(x*x-9)};
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=3.22:6.7] {1/(x*x-9)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,-0.111111)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below right] at (axis cs:0,-0.111111) {$\left(0,-\frac19\right)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above] at (axis cs:5,0) {$y=0$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The range is $\left(-\infty,-\dfrac19\right]\cup(0,\infty)$.

The domain is $\mathbb R$. Since $e^{-3x}>0$, the only intercept is $(0,0)$, and $f$ has the same sign as $x$.

$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=e^{-3x}-3xe^{-3x}\\&=(1-3x)e^{-3x}\\f''(x)&=-3e^{-3x}-3(1-3x)e^{-3x}\\&=(9x-6)e^{-3x}\end{aligned}$

For stationary points:

$\begin{aligned}1-3x&=0\\x&=\frac13\\f\left(\frac13\right)&=\frac{1}{3e}\\f''\left(\frac13\right)&=-\frac3e<0\end{aligned}$

Thus $\left(\dfrac13,\dfrac{1}{3e}\right)$ is a maximum.

For possible inflections:

$\begin{aligned}9x-6&=0\\x&=\frac23\\f\left(\frac23\right)&=\frac{2}{3e^2}\end{aligned}$

Since the exponential factor is positive, $f''$ changes from negative to positive at $x=\dfrac23$. The inflection is $\left(\dfrac23,\dfrac{2}{3e^2}\right)$.

As $x\to\infty$, $f(x)\to0^+$, so $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote on the right. As $x\to-\infty$, $f(x)\to-\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-0.5,xmax=2,ymin=-0.55,ymax=0.3,xtick={0.333333,0.666667},xticklabels={$\frac13$,$\frac23$},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=160]
\addplot[black,thick,<->,domain=-0.23:1.9] {x*exp(-3*x)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,0) (0.333333,0.122626) (0.666667,0.090224)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above left] at (axis cs:0.333333,0.122626) {$\left(\frac13,\frac{1}{3e}\right)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above right] at (axis cs:0.666667,0.090224) {$\left(\frac23,\frac{2}{3e^2}\right)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The domain is $x>0$. There is no $y$-intercept.

For the $x$-intercept:

$\begin{aligned}\ln(2x)&=0\\2x&=1\\x&=\frac12\end{aligned}$

Using the product rule:

$\begin{aligned}y'&=\ln(2x)+x\frac{2}{2x}\\&=\ln(2x)+1\\y''&=\frac1x\end{aligned}$

For the stationary point:

$\begin{aligned}\ln(2x)+1&=0\\\ln(2x)&=-1\\x&=\frac{1}{2e}\\y&=\frac{1}{2e}(-1)=-\frac{1}{2e}\end{aligned}$

Since $y''>0$ throughout the domain, $\left(\dfrac{1}{2e},-\dfrac{1}{2e}\right)$ is a minimum. The curve is concave up everywhere and has no point of inflection.

As $x\to0^+$, $y\to0^-$ and $y'\to-\infty$: the curve approaches the excluded origin with a tangent approaching vertical. As $x\to\infty$, $y\to\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-0.15,xmax=1.5,ymin=-0.45,ymax=1.6,xtick={0.5,1},xticklabels={$\frac12$,$1$},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=180]
\addplot[black,thick,->,domain=0.001:1.4] {x*ln(2*x)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,mark options={fill=white}] coordinates {(0,0)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0.183940,-0.183940) (0.5,0)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below right] at (axis cs:0.183940,-0.183940) {$\left(\frac{1}{2e},-\frac{1}{2e}\right)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above left] at (axis cs:0.5,0) {$\left(\frac12,0\right)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The range is $\left[-\dfrac{1}{2e},\infty\right)$.

The endpoints are $(0,0)$ and $(2\pi,\pi)$.

$\begin{aligned}y'&=\cos x+\frac12\\y''&=-\sin x\end{aligned}$

For stationary points:

$\begin{aligned}\cos x+\frac12&=0\\\cos x&=-\frac12\\x&=\frac{2\pi}{3},\ \frac{4\pi}{3}\end{aligned}$

At $x=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$, $y''=-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}<0$, giving a local maximum at $\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{3},\dfrac{\pi}{3}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$.

At $x=\dfrac{4\pi}{3}$, $y''=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}>0$, giving a local minimum at $\left(\dfrac{4\pi}{3},\dfrac{2\pi}{3}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$.

The only interior zero of $y''$ is $x=\pi$. The curve is concave down for $0<x<\pi$ and concave up for $\pi<x<2\pi$, so $\left(\pi,\dfrac\pi2\right)$ is a point of inflection. Its gradient is $y'(\pi)=-\dfrac12$, so it is non-horizontal.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-0.4,xmax=6.8,ymin=-0.4,ymax=3.7,xtick={2.094395,3.141593,4.188790,6.283185},xticklabels={$\frac{2\pi}{3}$,$\pi$,$\frac{4\pi}{3}$,$2\pi$},ytick=\empty,tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},samples=160]
\addplot[black,thick,domain=0:6.283185307] {sin(deg(x))+x/2};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,0) (2.094395,1.913223) (3.141593,1.570796) (4.188790,1.228370) (6.283185,3.141593)};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above] at (axis cs:2.094395,1.913223) {maximum};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,below] at (axis cs:4.188790,1.228370) {minimum};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above right] at (axis cs:3.141593,1.570796) {inflection};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,above left] at (axis cs:6.283185,3.141593) {$(2\pi,\pi)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The marked stationary points are local extrema. Comparing them with the endpoints, the absolute minimum is $(0,0)$ and the absolute maximum is $(2\pi,\pi)$.
